# TLS 1.3 peer-certificate policy — the one place that decides whether a server
# is trusted, and the reason the default client is no longer MITM-able.
#
# ## What was wrong
#
# Until this module existed, `tls13_connect(fd, hostname)` built its config from
# `tls13_default_client_config()`, whose `root_store` is `[]`, and the handshake
# ran chain verification only `if config.root_store.len() > 0`. So the default
# client verified *nothing*: it confirmed that the peer held the private key for
# whatever certificate the peer chose to send, which any attacker on the path can
# also do with a certificate they generated themselves. Hostname verification,
# added by the preceding increment, sat behind the same gate and was therefore
# also dead code for every default caller.
#
# The dangerous part was not the missing check. It was that "no trust anchors
# configured" and "verification deliberately disabled" were spelled the same way
# — an empty array — so the insecure state was the state you reached by not
# thinking about it.
#
# ## The rule now
#
# Verification is ON. There is exactly one way to turn it off, and it is a field
# whose name cannot be typed by accident or read as anything but a hazard:
#
#     Tls13ClientConfig.insecure_disable_certificate_verification: bool
#
# Defaults to `false`. When `false` (the default), a client that *cannot* verify
# refuses to connect:
#
#   - empty `root_store`  -> ERROR ("no trust anchors"), never a silent pass.
#     This is the whole point: absence of configuration is now a refusal.
#   - empty chain          -> ERROR.
#   - unparseable dates    -> ERROR (see validity.spl).
#   - no clock (`now < 0`) -> ERROR. A device with no time source must set the
#     insecure flag explicitly; it does not get a quiet exemption.
#
# When `true`, the client connects to anything and this module says so in one
# place, so `grep -rn insecure_disable_certificate_verification` is a complete
# audit of every caller that opted out.
#
# ## Ordering
#
# validity -> chain -> hostname. Validity first because it is pure parsing and
# needs no public-key operation, so an expired certificate is rejected before any
# signature work is done on attacker-supplied bytes. Every stage is fail-closed
# and the first failure short-circuits; there is no path through this function
# that returns `Ok` without all three having passed.
#
# ## Not covered here (filed, not silently implied)
#
# Revocation (CRL/OCSP/stapling), KeyUsage/EKU enforcement, and pathLenConstraint
# are still absent from the stack. This module makes the default *sound*, not
# complete; see .spipe/tls_default_verification/state.md for the remaining list.

use os.tls13.cert_verify.*

fn verify_peer_certificate(
    chain: [[u8]],
    root_store: [[u8]],
    hostname: text,
    now_unix: i64,
    insecure_disable_certificate_verification: bool
) -> CertVerifyResult:
    """Full peer-certificate policy: validity, chain to a trust anchor, hostname.

    Returns `Ok` only when all three pass, or when the caller has explicitly set
    `insecure_disable_certificate_verification`. An empty `root_store` is an
    error, not a bypass.
    """
    if insecure_disable_certificate_verification:
        return CertVerifyResult.Ok

    if chain.len() == 0:
        return CertVerifyResult.Err(msg: "server certificate chain is empty")

    if root_store.len() == 0:
        return CertVerifyResult.Err(msg: "TLS peer verification is enabled but root_store is empty: an empty trust store is not a request to skip verification. Supply trust anchors, or set insecure_disable_certificate_verification: true to opt out explicitly.")

    val time_result = verify_chain_validity(chain, now_unix)
    if val CertVerifyResult.Err(time_err) = time_result:
        return CertVerifyResult.Err(msg: time_err)

    val chain_result = verify_cert_chain(chain, root_store)
    if val CertChainResult.Err(chain_err) = chain_result:
        return CertVerifyResult.Err(msg: chain_err)

    # RFC 6125: a chain-valid certificate still says nothing about WHICH name it
    # was issued for. Without this a client accepts any CA-signed certificate for
    # any host, including one legitimately issued to the attacker.
    val name_result = verify_hostname(chain[0], hostname)
    if val CertVerifyResult.Err(name_err) = name_result:
        return CertVerifyResult.Err(msg: name_err)

    CertVerifyResult.Ok
